  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component implements a disclosure pattern but fails at the foundational level: it uses a non-interactive `<div>` instead of a native `<button>`, completely lacks keyboard activation, and omits all required ARIA state/association attributes. This is not an 80% implementation; it is a 0% WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern implementation. The design must be revised before testing or shipping.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\nBefore reading the code, I predicted:\n1. Non-native interactive element (`<div>` with `onClick`) breaking keyboard operability\n2. Missing `aria-expanded` state communication to assistive technology\n3. Missing `aria-controls` / `id` pairing between trigger and content panel\n4. Collapsed content exposed to screen readers (missing `aria-hidden=\"true\"`)\n5. Visual `+`/`\u2212` symbols announced redundantly by AT\n\nActual findings confirmed all predictions with additional structural gaps. No surprises; the component is fundamentally incomplete per the APG Disclosure pattern.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Non-interactive `<div>` used as trigger, breaking keyboard operability entirely**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:8` uses `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>` with no `role`, `tabIndex`, or keyboard event handlers.\n   - User group impacted: Keyboard-only users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern\n   - Fix: Replace `<div>` with `<button type=\"button\">`. Native buttons are keyboard-activated by default and announce as interactive. If a custom element is required for styling, add `role=\"button\" tabIndex={0} onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') toggle(); }}`, but native `<button>` is strongly preferred.\n\n2. **Missing `aria-expanded` state communication**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:8-12` toggles visual state via React `isExpanded` but never syncs it to the DOM for assistive technology.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern\n   - Fix: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the trigger element. Values must be strictly `\"true\"` or `\"false\"`.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Missing `aria-controls` / `id` association between trigger and content panel**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:8-12` (trigger) and `BuggyExpandable.jsx:15-18` (content) have no structural linkage.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern\n   - Fix: Add a stable `id` to the content `<div>` (e.g., `id={\\`content-\\${title}\\`}`) and reference it on the trigger via `aria-controls={contentId}`. This allows AT users to jump directly between trigger and panel.\n\n2. **Collapsed content not hidden from assistive technology**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:15-18` renders conditionally but lacks `aria-hidden` when `isExpanded` is false. When the component mounts, screen readers may announce content before it's visually visible, or announce it repeatedly on mount/unmount depending on framework behavior.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern\n   - Fix: Add `aria-hidden={!isExpanded}` to the content `<div>`. When collapsed, this removes it from the accessibility tree. Ensure focus remains on the trigger after toggle (standard behavior).\n\n3. **Visual `+`/`\u2212` symbols exposed to screen readers without suppression**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:11-12` renders `{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}` directly in the DOM. Screen readers announce these as \"plus\" or \"minus\", creating redundant/confusing announcements alongside `aria-expanded`.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users\n   - WCAG/APG Citation: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - Fix: Wrap symbols in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`. The state is already communicated programmatically via `aria-expanded`; visual symbols should be hidden from AT.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Focus indicator styling not defined in component**: While CSS handles this, the component doesn't ensure focus visibility meets WCAG 2.4.13 (2px perimeter, 3:1 contrast change). Add a note to the design system or include `focus-visible` polyfill/styling guidelines in component docs.\n- **No `aria-label` fallback for empty titles**: If `title` prop is omitted or empty, the button has no accessible name. Add `aria-label={title || 'Expand section'}` as a safeguard.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Focus management on toggle**: Per APG Disclosure pattern, focus should remain on the trigger after expansion/collapse. React's default behavior keeps focus on the clicked element, which is correct here. Document this expectation for consumers of the component.\n- **Semantic landmark structure**: The wrapper `<div className=\"expandable-section\">` could be a `<section>` or `<fieldset>` if part of a larger form/group, but `<div>` is acceptable for standalone disclosure.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Native `<button>` element replacing non-semantic `<div>`\n- `aria-expanded` state synchronization with DOM\n- `aria-controls` / `id` cross-reference between trigger and panel\n- `aria-hidden` on collapsed content to prevent AT exposure\n- `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on visual `+`/`\u2212` symbols\n- Keyboard event handling (`Enter`/`Space`) if `<button>` is not used\n- Focus indicator compliance (CSS dependency, not in component scope)\n- Empty title fallback for accessible name\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | No `aria-expanded`, no `aria-controls`, content exposed when collapsed, symbols announced redundantly |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | `<div>` with only `onClick` = completely unactivatable via keyboard |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Relies entirely on CSS for clickability; focus indicator not defined in component |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | State not programmatically announced; interaction model unclear without visual cues |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations/motion present |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | No color-only state indicators detected |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: \nThe component fails foundational accessibility requirements for interactive widgets. Keyboard users cannot activate it at all (CRITICAL). Screen reader users receive no structural context, state announcements, or panel association (MAJOR). The design does not match the WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern in any measurable way. This is not a rubber-stamp pass; it is a complete pattern gap. Severity calibrated via Realist Check: keyboard blockage and missing AT state are production-blocking for assistive technology users. No downgrade applied.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent application provide global CSS that ensures focus visibility on `.expandable-trigger`? If not, focus will be invisible to keyboard users.\n- Is this component used inside a `<form>` or `<fieldset>`? If so, additional grouping semantics may be required.\n- Are there multiple instances of this component on one page? If so, `id` generation must be unique (e.g., using a counter or UUID) to avoid `aria-controls` collisions.",
